  <p>
    A spherical balloon is being inflated at a constant rate of <m>20</m> cubic inches per second.  We are interested in using calculus to understand questions such as: how fast is the radius of the balloon changing at the instant the balloon's diameter is <m>12</m> inches? is the radius changing more rapidly when <m>d = 12</m> or when <m>d = 16</m>? 
  </p>
  <p>

    <ol marker="a.">
      <li>
        <p>
          On a sheet of paper, draw several spheres that reflect spheres of different volume.  As the balloon is being inflated, what attributes of the sphere change?  Check all that apply.
          <answer matchPartial selectMultiple>
            <shortDescription>various quantities of the sphere which might be changing</shortDescription>
              <choice credit="1">the radius of the sphere</choice>
              <choice credit="1">the surface area of the sphere</choice>
              <choice>the shape of the sphere</choice>
              <choice credit="1">the diameter of the sphere</choice>
              <choice credit="1">the volume of the sphere</choice>
          </answer>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Recall that the volume of a sphere of radius <m>r</m> is <m>V = \frac{4}{3} \pi r^3</m>.  Note that in the setting of this problem, <em>both</em> <m>V</m> and <m>r</m> are changing as time <m>t</m> changes, and thus both <m>V</m> and <m>r</m> may be viewed as implicit functions of <m>t</m>, with respective derivatives <m>\frac{dV}{dt}</m> and <m>\frac{dr}{dt}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Implicitly differentiate both sides of the equation <m>V = \frac{4}{3} \pi r^3</m> with respect to <m>t</m> (using the chain rule on the right) to find a formula for <m>\frac{dV}{dt}</m> that depends on both <m>r</m> and <m>\frac{dr}{dt}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <answer symbolicEquality simplifyOnCompare><label><m>\frac{dV}{dt} = </m> </label>4 pi r^2 dr/dt</answer>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          At this point in our work, by differentiating we have <q>related the rates</q> of change of <m>V</m> and <m>r</m>.  Recall that we are given that the balloon is being inflated at a constant <em>rate</em> of 20 cubic inches per second.
        </p>
        <p>
          This information tells us that <answer><choiceInput inline><shortDescription>how the rate of inflation is described using notation</shortDescription>
            <choice>?</choice>
            <choice><m>\frac{dr}{dt}=20</m></choice>
            <choice credit="1"><m>\frac{dV}{dt}=20</m></choice>
            <choice><m>\frac{dS}{dt}=20</m></choice>
          </choiceInput></answer>  because <answer><choiceInput inline><label>the units on volume in this context are </label>
            <choice>?</choice>
            <choice credit="1">cubic inches</choice>
            <choice>cubic inches per second</choice>
            <choice>inches</choice>
            <choice>square inches</choice>
          </choiceInput></answer>, so the given units of <q>20 cubic inches per second</q> tell us this must be the rate of change of volume with respect to time.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          From part (c), we know the value of <m>\frac{dV}{dt}</m> at every value of <m>t</m>. Next, observe that when the diameter of the balloon is 12, we know the value of the radius.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the equation <m>\frac{dV}{dt} = 4\pi r^2 \frac{dr}{dt}</m>, substitute these values for the relevant quantities and solve for the remaining unknown quantity, which is <m>\frac{dr}{dt}</m>. How fast is the radius changing at the instant <m>d = 12</m>?
        </p>
        <p>
          <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" allowedErrorIsAbsolute><label><m>\left.\frac{dr}{dt}\right|_{d = 12} = </m> </label><number>20/(4 * pi * 6^2)</number></answer>, and the units on this quantity are <answer><choiceInput inline><shortDescription>units on the rate of change of radius</shortDescription>
          <choice>?</choice>
          <choice>inches</choice>
          <choice credit="1">inches per second</choice>
          <choice>seconds per inch</choice>
          <choice>seconds</choice>
          </choiceInput></answer>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          How is the situation different when <m>d = 16</m>?
          When is the radius changing more rapidly,
          when <m>d = 12</m> or when <m>d = 16</m>?
        </p>
          <p>
            <answer>
                <choiceInput inline="true"><label>The radius is changing more rapidly when <m>d =</m></label>
                  <choice>?</choice>
                  <choice credit="1">12</choice>
                  <choice>16</choice>
                  <choice>not enough information to say</choice>
                </choiceInput>
            </answer>
          </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>